        <line x1="32" y1="8"PROJECT OVERVIEW

Project Title: D.S-Grok: A Game-Based Learning Web Application for Data Structures Education

Project Type: Undergraduate Computer Science Senior Project / Dissertation

University: American University of Cyprus (AUCY)

Application Type: Web-based educational platform

Purpose:
To investigate whether Game-Based Learning (GBL) principles can be used to improve the learning experience of Data Structures by combining:

Interactive visualizations
Practical challenges
Quizzes
Progress tracking
Gamification elements

within a single web application.

IMPORTANT POSITIONING

The application should be described as:

"A Game-Based Learning application supported by gamification elements."

Do NOT describe it as purely gamified.

Explanation:

Game-Based Learning Components
Interactive pointer visualizer
Interactive linked list visualizer
Lesson activities
Linked list traversal challenge
Linked list operations challenge
Interactive quizzes
Gamification Components
XP
Achievements
Levels
Progression
Leaderboard
Rewards

Recommended dissertation/presentation statement:

D.S-Grok combines game-based learning activities with gamification mechanics to support both educational outcomes and learner motivation.

RESEARCH AIM

The aim of this project was:

To investigate the application of Game-Based Learning within Data Structures education and develop a web-based educational platform that supports learning through visualization, interaction, assessment, and progression mechanisms.

RESEARCH QUESTIONS
What challenges do students face when learning Data Structures?
Can visualization and interactive learning activities improve understanding of abstract concepts?
How can Game-Based Learning principles be integrated into a web-based educational platform?
QUESTIONNAIRE STUDY

Participants:

21 Computer Science students

Distribution:

Year 1: 4.8%
Year 2: 47.6%
Year 3: 28.6%
Year 4: 19.1%

Sampling:

Convenience Sampling

Data Collection:

Google Forms

Analysis:

Descriptive Statistics
IMPORTANT FINDING

Students identified:

Most Difficult Topics

Graphs received the highest score.

HOWEVER:

Graphs are not formally covered in the Data Structures module taught to the target population.

Therefore:

Pointers and Memory Management

followed by

Linked Lists

were selected because they:

Ranked highly
Are part of the curriculum
Were repeatedly mentioned by students
